{"causes": ["The oral transmission of Buddhist teachings necessitated a formalized written canon to preserve its integrity.", "The political stability under King Vattagamani in Sri Lanka allowed for the compilation and standardization of Buddhist scriptures.", "The schisms within early Buddhism prompted the need for a unified text to unify various sects and teachings."], "effects": ["The Tripitaka became the authoritative scripture for Theravada Buddhism, shaping its practices and beliefs.", "It facilitated the spread of Buddhism into Southeast Asia, influencing cultural and religious developments in countries like Thailand and Myanmar.", "The canon's teachings on ethics and meditation have permeated modern spiritual practices and influenced various forms of psychotherapy."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "buddhism", "sourceName": "Buddhism", "verb": "CANONIZES", "targetSlug": "tripitaka", "targetName": "Tripitaka", "context": "The Tripitaka serves as the canonical collection that defines the teachings and practices of Buddhism."}, {"sourceSlug": "vattagamani", "sourceName": "King Vattagamani", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "tripitaka", "targetName": "Tripitaka", "context": "King Vattagamani initiated the Fourth Buddhist Council around 1st century BCE, leading to the compilation of the Tripitaka."}, {"sourceSlug": "pali-canon", "sourceName": "Pāli Canon", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "tripitaka", "targetName": "Tripitaka", "context": "The Tripitaka is often identified as the Pāli Canon, the oldest complete collection of Buddhist scriptures."}, {"sourceSlug": "sri-lanka", "sourceName": "Sri Lanka", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "tripitaka", "targetName": "Tripitaka", "context": "The Tripitaka was compiled in Sri Lanka during the Fourth Buddhist Council in the 1st century BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "theravada-buddhism", "sourceName": "Theravada Buddhism", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "tripitaka", "targetName": "Tripitaka", "context": "Theravada Buddhism is heavily influenced by the teachings and structure presented in the Tripitaka."}], "places": [{"name": "Anuradhapura, Sri Lanka", "role": "Compilation site"}, {"name": "Bodh Gaya, India", "role": "Buddha's enlightenment"}, {"name": "Kandy, Sri Lanka", "role": "Cultural center"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Tripitaka", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "tripitaka"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1321432", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buddhist_canons"], "tags": ["religious text", "Buddhist Scriptures (Pali Canon, Sutras)", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Tripitaka left a notable mark on Classical history, documented across 70 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The Tripitaka codified the core teachings of Buddhism, influencing millions of followers and shaping religious practices across Southeast Asia, which in turn contributed to the development of diverse Buddhist traditions.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-28T17:05:11Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#30381001829", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "There are several Buddhist canons, which refers to the various scriptural collections of Buddhist sacred scriptures or the various Buddhist scriptural canons. Some of these collections are also called Tipiṭaka or Tripiṭaka, meaning "Triple Basket", a traditional term for the three main divisions of some ancient canons. In ancient India, there were several Buddhist scriptural canons that were organized into three main textual divisions: Vinaya, Sutra and Abhidharma. For example, the Pāli Tipiṭaka is composed of the Vinaya Piṭaka, the Sutta Piṭaka, and the Abhidhamma Piṭaka.", "newValue": "The Tripitaka, often referred to as the Pāli Canon, represents the foundational scripture of Theravada Buddhism, compiled around the 1st century BCE in ancient India. The term 'Tripitaka' translates to 'Three Baskets,' encompassing three distinct collections: the Vinaya Pitaka, which outlines monastic rules; the Sutta Pitaka, containing the discourses of the Buddha; and the Abhidhamma Pitaka, which delves into philosophical and doctrinal analysis. Its significance lies in its role as a comprehensive repository of Buddhist teachings, influencing religious practices and philosophical thought across Asia and beyond.

The compilation of the Tripitaka occurred during the Fourth Buddhist Council, convened in Sri Lanka under King Vattagamani, where the oral traditions were transcribed into written form to preserve the teachings amid growing schisms. This scriptural canon not only codified the ethical and doctrinal frameworks of Buddhist monastic life but also facilitated the spread of Buddhism across regions, including Southeast Asia, where it became a central religious text. The Tripitaka has undergone various translations and adaptations, further enriching its role in the global religious and cultural landscape.

The legacy of the Tripitaka extends beyond its immediate religious context; it has served as a critical source of knowledge for scholars studying early Indian philosophy, ethics, and psychology. Its influence permeated cultural and intellectual developments, spawning various schools of Buddhist thought, including Zen and Tibetan Buddhism, which interpret the texts in diverse ways. Furthermore, its emphasis on meditation and ethical conduct resonates in contemporary spiritual practices, reflecting its enduring relevance."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-28T17:05:11Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#30381001829", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": ["Religious devotion and spiritual expression"], "newValue": ["The oral transmission of Buddhist teachings necessitated a formalized written canon to preserve its integrity.", "The political stability under King Vattagamani in Sri Lanka allowed for the compilation and standardization of Buddhist scriptures.", "The schisms within early Buddhism prompted the need for a unified text to unify various sects and teachings."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-28T17:05:11Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#30381001829", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": ["Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission"], "newValue": ["The Tripitaka became the authoritative scripture for Theravada Buddhism, shaping its practices and beliefs.", "It facilitated the spread of Buddhism into Southeast Asia, influencing cultural and religious developments in countries like Thailand and Myanmar.", "The canon's teachings on ethics and meditation have permeated modern spiritual practices and influenced various forms of psychotherapy."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-28T17:05:11Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#30381001829", "field": "detailsJson.relationships", "oldValue": [{"sourceSlug": "tripitaka", "sourceName": "Tripitaka", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "era-classical", "targetName": "Classical", "context": "Tripitaka is situate